# Egan's Fundamentals of Respiratory Care (Elsevier).
# Chapter list: 13th edition, from the publisher's table of contents (us.elsevierhealth.com).
# Summaries cover each chapter's standard topics; they are written for this site, not copied from the book.

def ch(n, title, text, keys):
    return (n, title, [text], keys)

C = [
    ch(1, "Early History of Respiratory Care", "Respiratory care grew from early oxygen therapy and inhalation treatments. Oxygen was discovered in the 1770s (Priestley, Scheele; named by Lavoisier). The polio epidemics brought the iron lung (negative-pressure ventilation), and positive-pressure ventilators followed. The profession formed in the 1940s as 'inhalation therapy' and is represented today by the AARC.", ["Oxygen discovered in the 1770s", "Iron lung = negative pressure", "Inhalation therapy became respiratory care", "AARC founded 1947"]),
    ch(2, "The Profession of Respiratory Care", "Respiratory therapists (RTs) assess, treat, and educate patients with heart and lung disorders. Credentials are given by the NBRC: CRT (entry level, via the TMC exam) and RRT (advanced, via the TMC high cut score and the Clinical Simulation Exam). CoARC accredits programs, and states license RTs. The AARC is the professional society.", ["NBRC gives CRT and RRT", "CoARC accredits programs", "AARC = professional society", "States license RTs"]),
    ch(3, "Quality, Patient Safety, and Communication", "Quality care uses protocols, evidence-based practice, and quality improvement. Patient safety includes two patient identifiers, preventing falls and errors, and electrical and fire safety (oxygen supports combustion). Good communication, including SBAR handoffs and teach-back, reduces errors.", ["Use two patient identifiers", "SBAR for handoffs", "Oxygen supports fire", "Protocols guide therapy"]),
    ch(4, "Principles of Infection Prevention and Control", "Infections spread by contact, droplet, and airborne routes. Hand hygiene is the single most important prevention method. Standard precautions apply to all patients; transmission-based precautions add contact, droplet, or airborne measures (N95 and negative-pressure rooms for TB). Equipment is cleaned, disinfected, or sterilized, and ventilator-associated pneumonia (VAP) bundles reduce infection.", ["Hand hygiene is most important", "Airborne: N95 + negative pressure", "Standard precautions for all", "VAP bundle: head of bed 30°+"]),
    ch(5, "Ethical and Legal Implications of Practice", "Ethical principles are autonomy, beneficence, nonmaleficence, justice, veracity, confidentiality, and role fidelity. Legal issues include negligence and malpractice, HIPAA privacy, informed consent, scope of practice, and documentation.", ["Autonomy and informed consent", "Negligence and malpractice", "HIPAA confidentiality", "Work within scope of practice"]),
    ch(6, "Physical Principles of Respiratory Care", "Gas laws explain respiratory care: Boyle's law (pressure and volume are inversely related), Charles's law (volume and temperature), Gay-Lussac's law, and Dalton's law (total pressure equals the sum of partial pressures). Other principles include humidity, the Bernoulli and Venturi effects, laminar vs. turbulent flow, Poiseuille's law, and surface tension (Laplace's law).", ["Boyle: P and V inverse", "Dalton: partial pressures add up", "Venturi principle entrains air", "Laplace: surface tension in alveoli"]),
    ch(7, "E-Medicine in Respiratory Care", "Technology supports care through electronic health records, telehealth and remote monitoring, computerized protocols, and clinical decision support. RTs must protect patient data and verify information sources.", ["EHRs", "Telehealth and remote monitoring", "Clinical decision support", "Protect patient data"]),
    ch(8, "Fundamentals of Respiratory Care Research", "Evidence-based practice relies on research. Designs include randomized controlled trials (the strongest single study), cohort, case-control, and descriptive studies; systematic reviews and meta-analyses rank highest. Key concepts include hypotheses, variables, statistical significance (p < 0.05), and ethics oversight by IRBs.", ["RCTs are strong evidence", "Meta-analyses rank highest", "p < 0.05 = significant", "IRB approves research"]),
    ch(9, "The Respiratory System", "The upper airway (nose, pharynx, larynx) warms, humidifies, and filters air. The lower airway branches from the trachea (carina at about T4–T5) into bronchi, bronchioles, and alveoli, where gas exchange happens. The right mainstem bronchus is straighter, so aspirated objects and deep tubes go right. The diaphragm (phrenic nerve, C3–C5) is the main breathing muscle.", ["Right mainstem more vertical", "Alveoli = gas exchange", "Diaphragm: phrenic nerve C3–C5", "Upper airway warms, humidifies, filters"]),
    ch(10, "The Cardiovascular System", "The heart pumps blood through pulmonary and systemic circuits. Cardiac output = heart rate × stroke volume (normal about 4–8 L/min). Stroke volume depends on preload, afterload, and contractility. The conduction system runs from the SA node to the AV node, bundle of His, bundle branches, and Purkinje fibers.", ["CO = HR × SV", "Preload, afterload, contractility", "SA node is the pacemaker", "Right heart → lungs"]),
    ch(11, "Ventilation", "Ventilation moves air in and out. Inspiration is active (diaphragm contracts); normal exhalation is passive. Compliance is how easily the lungs stretch; resistance opposes airflow. Minute ventilation = tidal volume × rate. Dead space doesn't take part in gas exchange; alveolar ventilation does.", ["Inspiration active, exhalation passive", "Compliance = stretchiness", "VE = VT × f", "Dead space vs. alveolar ventilation"]),
    ch(12, "Gas Exchange and Transport", "Oxygen and CO2 diffuse across the alveolar-capillary membrane. Most oxygen is carried bound to hemoglobin; the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve shifts right (easier release) with acidosis, high CO2, fever, and high 2,3-DPG. Most CO2 travels as bicarbonate. V/Q mismatch and shunt cause hypoxemia.", ["Most O2 carried on hemoglobin", "Right shift = easier O2 release", "Most CO2 as bicarbonate", "Shunt and V/Q mismatch → hypoxemia"]),
    ch(13, "Solutions, Body Fluids, and Electrolytes", "Body water is divided into intracellular and extracellular compartments. Key electrolytes include sodium (main extracellular cation), potassium (main intracellular cation), chloride, and bicarbonate. Imbalances such as hypokalemia can cause arrhythmias and muscle weakness. IV solutions can be isotonic, hypotonic, or hypertonic.", ["Na+ main extracellular cation", "K+ main intracellular cation", "Hypokalemia → arrhythmias", "Isotonic, hypotonic, hypertonic"]),
    ch(14, "Acid-Base Balance", "Normal arterial pH is 7.35–7.45, PaCO2 35–45 mm Hg, and HCO3- 22–26 mEq/L. Respiratory acidosis comes from hypoventilation (high PaCO2); respiratory alkalosis from hyperventilation. Metabolic acidosis (low HCO3-) occurs with DKA, lactic acidosis, or renal failure; metabolic alkalosis with vomiting or diuretics. The lungs and kidneys compensate.", ["pH 7.35–7.45", "PaCO2 35–45; HCO3- 22–26", "High CO2 = respiratory acidosis", "Kidneys compensate slowly"]),
    ch(15, "Regulation of Breathing", "Breathing is controlled by the medulla and pons. Central chemoreceptors respond mainly to CO2 (via pH of cerebrospinal fluid); peripheral chemoreceptors in the carotid and aortic bodies respond mainly to low PaO2. Reflexes include the Hering-Breuer inflation reflex. Abnormal patterns include Cheyne-Stokes, Kussmaul, and Biot breathing.", ["Medulla controls breathing", "Central chemoreceptors → CO2", "Peripheral chemoreceptors → low O2", "Kussmaul = deep, fast (acidosis)"]),
    ch(16, "Bedside Assessment of the Patient", "Assessment includes the interview (dyspnea, cough, sputum, chest pain) and physical exam: inspection, palpation, percussion, and auscultation. Normal vitals: pulse 60–100, respirations 12–20, SpO2 over 95%. Breath sounds include crackles (fluid, opening alveoli), wheezes (narrowed airways), rhonchi (secretions), and stridor (upper airway obstruction).", ["Inspect, palpate, percuss, auscultate", "Wheezes = narrowed airways", "Stridor = upper airway emergency", "Crackles = fluid/atelectasis"]),
    ch(17, "Interpreting Clinical and Laboratory Data", "Key labs include the CBC (hemoglobin, WBCs for infection), electrolytes, BUN and creatinine (kidney function), cardiac markers (troponin), BNP (heart failure), and sputum Gram stain and culture. Results guide diagnosis and therapy.", ["CBC: Hgb, WBC", "Troponin = heart damage", "BNP = heart failure", "Sputum culture identifies organisms"]),
    ch(18, "Interpreting the Electrocardiogram", "The ECG shows the P wave (atrial depolarization), QRS complex (ventricular depolarization), and T wave (ventricular repolarization). Normal sinus rhythm is 60–100/min. Dysrhythmias include sinus tachycardia and bradycardia, atrial fibrillation, PVCs, ventricular tachycardia, and ventricular fibrillation (defibrillate).", ["P = atria; QRS = ventricles", "T wave = repolarization", "V-fib → defibrillate", "A-fib: irregular, no P waves"]),
    ch(19, "Analysis and Monitoring of Gas Exchange", "Arterial blood gases measure pH, PaCO2, PaO2 (normal 80–100 mm Hg), HCO3-, and SaO2. The radial artery is preferred; perform the modified Allen test first. Pulse oximetry (SpO2) is noninvasive but unreliable with carbon monoxide poisoning. Capnography measures exhaled CO2 (PetCO2) and confirms tube placement.", ["PaO2 80–100 mm Hg", "Modified Allen test before radial stick", "SpO2 falsely high with CO poisoning", "Capnography confirms ET tube"]),
    ch(20, "Pulmonary Function Testing", "Spirometry measures FVC, FEV1, and the FEV1/FVC ratio. Obstructive disease (asthma, COPD) has a low FEV1/FVC ratio (under 70%); restrictive disease (fibrosis) has reduced lung volumes with a normal or high ratio. Other tests include lung volumes (TLC, RV), DLCO (diffusing capacity), and bronchodilator response.", ["FEV1/FVC < 70% = obstruction", "Restrictive = low TLC", "DLCO measures diffusion", "Bronchodilator response in asthma"]),
    ch(21, "Review of Thoracic Imaging", "Chest X-rays (PA view preferred; AP for bedside) show the lungs, heart, and tubes. White (radiopaque) areas show dense tissue or fluid; dark (radiolucent) areas show air. Findings include infiltrates, pneumothorax, pleural effusion, and atelectasis. The ET tube tip should be 3–5 cm above the carina. CT and ultrasound add detail.", ["PA view preferred", "Air is dark; fluid is white", "ET tip 3–5 cm above carina", "CT gives detail"]),
    ch(22, "Flexible Bronchoscopy and the Respiratory Therapist", "Flexible bronchoscopy looks inside the airways to diagnose (biopsy, bronchoalveolar lavage) and treat (remove secretions or foreign bodies). RTs assist with preparation, sedation monitoring, oxygen, and specimen handling. Complications include hypoxemia, bleeding, and pneumothorax.", ["Diagnostic and therapeutic", "BAL collects samples", "Monitor SpO2 during procedure", "Risks: hypoxemia, bleeding"]),
    ch(23, "Nutrition Assessment", "Malnutrition weakens breathing muscles and immunity. Assessment includes weight, BMI, albumin and prealbumin, and diet history. Indirect calorimetry measures energy needs; the respiratory quotient (RQ, about 0.8 normally) shows fuel use. Overfeeding carbohydrates raises CO2 production.", ["Malnutrition weakens respiratory muscles", "Indirect calorimetry measures energy needs", "RQ ~0.8", "Excess carbs → more CO2"]),
    ch(24, "Pneumonia", "Pneumonia is infection of the lung tissue, classified as community-acquired, hospital-acquired, or ventilator-associated. Common causes include Streptococcus pneumoniae (the most common bacterial cause), viruses, and aspiration. Signs: fever, cough, crackles, infiltrate on X-ray. Treatment includes antibiotics, oxygen, and airway clearance; vaccines prevent some cases.", ["S. pneumoniae most common", "CAP, HAP, VAP", "Infiltrate on X-ray", "Vaccines prevent"]),
    ch(25, "Obstructive Lung Disease: COPD, Asthma, and Related Diseases", "COPD (emphysema and chronic bronchitis) is mainly caused by smoking; smoking cessation is the most important intervention. Asthma is reversible airway inflammation and bronchospasm treated with bronchodilators and inhaled steroids. Related diseases include bronchiectasis and cystic fibrosis. COPD patients with chronic CO2 retention need careful oxygen titration.", ["Smoking cessation most important", "Asthma is reversible", "Inhaled steroids control inflammation", "Titrate O2 carefully in CO2 retainers"]),
    ch(26, "Interstitial Lung Disease", "Interstitial lung diseases inflame and scar the lung tissue, causing restrictive patterns, reduced DLCO, dry crackles, and dyspnea. Causes include idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis, occupational exposures (asbestosis, silicosis), sarcoidosis, and drug reactions.", ["Restrictive pattern", "Reduced DLCO", "Asbestosis and silicosis", "Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis"]),
    ch(27, "Pleural Diseases", "Pleural effusion is fluid in the pleural space (transudate or exudate), treated with thoracentesis. Pneumothorax is air in the pleural space; tension pneumothorax (tracheal shift away, hypotension) is an emergency treated with needle decompression and a chest tube. Empyema is pus in the pleural space.", ["Effusion → thoracentesis", "Tension pneumothorax is an emergency", "Chest tube drains air/fluid", "Empyema = pus"]),
    ch(28, "Pulmonary Vascular Disease", "Pulmonary embolism, usually from a deep vein thrombosis, causes sudden dyspnea, tachycardia, and hypoxemia; it's diagnosed by CT angiography and treated with anticoagulants. Pulmonary hypertension raises pressure in the pulmonary arteries and can cause cor pulmonale (right heart failure).", ["PE usually from DVT", "CT angiography diagnoses PE", "Anticoagulants treat", "Cor pulmonale = right heart failure"]),
    ch(29, "Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome", "ARDS is acute, noncardiogenic pulmonary edema with severe hypoxemia (PaO2/FiO2 ratio of 300 or less), bilateral infiltrates, and stiff lungs. Causes include sepsis, pneumonia, and trauma. Treatment uses lung-protective ventilation (tidal volume 4–8 mL/kg ideal body weight, plateau pressure under 30 cm H2O), PEEP, and prone positioning.", ["P/F ratio ≤ 300", "Low tidal volume 4–8 mL/kg IBW", "Plateau pressure < 30", "PEEP and prone positioning"]),
    ch(30, "Respiratory Management of Trauma, Obesity, Near Drowning, and Burns", "Chest trauma includes flail chest, rib fractures, and pulmonary contusion. Obesity reduces lung volumes and causes hypoventilation. Drowning causes hypoxemia and aspiration. Burns and smoke inhalation can cause airway swelling (consider early intubation) and carbon monoxide poisoning (treat with 100% oxygen or hyperbaric oxygen).", ["Flail chest: paradoxical movement", "Smoke inhalation → early intubation", "CO poisoning → 100% O2", "Obesity hypoventilation"]),
    ch(31, "Acute Heart Failure", "Left heart failure causes pulmonary edema (crackles, dyspnea, pink frothy sputum, orthopnea); right heart failure causes peripheral edema and jugular venous distension. Treatment includes oxygen, diuretics, vasodilators, and CPAP or noninvasive ventilation.", ["Left HF → pulmonary edema", "Right HF → peripheral edema, JVD", "CPAP helps cardiogenic edema", "Diuretics remove fluid"]),
    ch(32, "Lung Cancer", "Lung cancer is the leading cause of cancer death, mostly caused by smoking. Types are non-small cell (adenocarcinoma, squamous cell, large cell) and small cell (aggressive). Screening with low-dose CT is recommended for high-risk smokers. Treatment includes surgery, chemotherapy, radiation, and targeted therapy.", ["Leading cancer death", "NSCLC vs. small cell", "Low-dose CT screening", "Smoking is main cause"]),
    ch(33, "Neuromuscular and Other Diseases of the Chest Wall", "Neuromuscular diseases such as Guillain-Barré syndrome, myasthenia gravis, ALS, and muscular dystrophy weaken breathing muscles. Monitor vital capacity and maximal inspiratory pressure (MIP/NIF); declining values signal need for ventilatory support. Chest wall disorders include kyphoscoliosis.", ["Monitor VC and MIP", "Guillain-Barré: ascending weakness", "Myasthenia gravis", "Kyphoscoliosis restricts"]),
    ch(34, "Disorders of Sleep", "Obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) is airway collapse during sleep, causing snoring, apneas, and daytime sleepiness. It's diagnosed by polysomnography and measured by the apnea-hypopnea index (AHI). CPAP is the main treatment. Central sleep apnea involves lack of respiratory effort.", ["OSA = airway collapse", "Polysomnography diagnoses", "AHI measures severity", "CPAP is main treatment"]),
    ch(35, "Neonatal and Pediatric Respiratory Disorders", "Neonatal disorders include respiratory distress syndrome (surfactant deficiency in premature infants; treat with surfactant), transient tachypnea of the newborn, meconium aspiration, and bronchopulmonary dysplasia. Pediatric disorders include croup (steeple sign, barking cough), epiglottitis (thumb sign; emergency), bronchiolitis (often RSV), and cystic fibrosis.", ["RDS → surfactant", "Croup: steeple sign", "Epiglottitis: thumb sign, emergency", "Bronchiolitis often RSV"]),
    ch(36, "Airway Pharmacology", "Bronchodilators include short-acting beta-2 agonists (albuterol, a rescue drug), long-acting beta-agonists, and anticholinergics (ipratropium, tiotropium). Anti-inflammatory drugs include inhaled corticosteroids and leukotriene modifiers. Mucolytics (acetylcysteine) thin secretions. Side effects of beta-agonists include tachycardia and tremor.", ["Albuterol = rescue SABA", "Anticholinergics: ipratropium", "Inhaled steroids control asthma", "Beta-agonists → tachycardia, tremor"]),
    ch(37, "Airway Management", "Airway care includes suctioning (limit to 10–15 seconds; preoxygenate), artificial airways (oropharyngeal, nasopharyngeal, endotracheal, tracheostomy), and intubation. Confirm ET tube placement with capnography, breath sounds, and chest X-ray. Cuff pressure is kept at 20–30 cm H2O.", ["Suction ≤ 15 seconds", "Confirm tube with capnography", "Cuff pressure 20–30 cm H2O", "Preoxygenate before suctioning"]),
    ch(38, "Emergency Cardiovascular Life Support", "BLS and ACLS include high-quality CPR (rate 100–120/min, depth at least 2 inches in adults, full recoil), early defibrillation for VF and pulseless VT, airway management, and drugs such as epinephrine and amiodarone. Capnography monitors CPR quality and return of spontaneous circulation.", ["CPR 100–120/min", "Defibrillate VF/pulseless VT", "Epinephrine in arrest", "Rising PetCO2 suggests ROSC"]),
    ch(39, "Humidity and Bland Aerosol Therapy", "Humidity prevents drying of the airway, especially when the upper airway is bypassed. Devices include heated humidifiers and heat and moisture exchangers (HMEs). Bland aerosols (sterile water or saline) help with upper airway swelling and sputum induction (hypertonic saline).", ["Bypassed airway needs humidity", "HME = 'artificial nose'", "Heated humidifiers", "Hypertonic saline for sputum induction"]),
    ch(40, "Aerosol Drug Therapy", "Aerosol delivery devices include metered-dose inhalers (use with a spacer), dry powder inhalers (need a strong, fast inhalation), small-volume nebulizers, and soft mist inhalers. Particle size matters: 1–5 microns reach the lower airways. Patient technique is key.", ["MDI + spacer", "DPI needs fast inhalation", "1–5 micron particles reach lower airways", "Teach technique"]),
    ch(41, "Storage and Delivery of Medical Gases", "Medical gases are stored in color-coded cylinders (U.S.: oxygen green, nitrous oxide blue, CO2 gray, helium brown) and bulk liquid systems. Safety systems prevent wrong connections: PISS (pin index, small cylinders), DISS, and quick-connects. Regulators reduce pressure and flowmeters control flow.", ["Oxygen cylinders green (U.S.)", "PISS for small cylinders", "DISS for larger connections", "Secure cylinders upright"]),
    ch(42, "Medical Gas Therapy", "Oxygen treats hypoxemia. Low-flow devices (nasal cannula 1–6 L/min, about 24–44% FiO2) and high-flow devices (Venturi masks for precise FiO2, high-flow nasal cannula) are used. Nonrebreather masks give the highest FiO2 of simple devices. Hazards include oxygen toxicity, fire, absorption atelectasis, and retinopathy of prematurity. Heliox and nitric oxide are specialty gases.", ["Nasal cannula 1–6 L/min", "Venturi = precise FiO2", "Nonrebreather = high FiO2", "Oxygen toxicity and fire hazards"]),
    ch(43, "Lung Expansion Therapy", "Lung expansion therapy prevents and treats atelectasis. Incentive spirometry (sustained maximal inspiration) is first choice for alert patients. Others include intermittent positive-pressure breathing (IPPB), CPAP, and deep breathing. Post-op patients benefit most.", ["Incentive spirometry first", "Prevents atelectasis", "IPPB and CPAP", "Post-op patients"]),
    ch(44, "Airway Clearance Therapy", "Airway clearance helps remove secretions in conditions like cystic fibrosis and bronchiectasis. Techniques include postural drainage and chest percussion, directed coughing (huff cough), positive expiratory pressure (PEP), oscillating devices (flutter), high-frequency chest wall oscillation (vest), and active cycle of breathing.", ["Postural drainage and percussion", "Huff cough", "PEP and flutter devices", "HFCWO vest"]),
    ch(45, "Respiratory Failure and the Need for Ventilatory Support", "Hypoxemic respiratory failure (type I) is PaO2 under 60 mm Hg despite oxygen; hypercapnic (type II) is PaCO2 over 50 mm Hg with acidosis. Signs needing ventilation include apnea, severe acidosis, fatigue, and inability to protect the airway.", ["Type I: PaO2 < 60", "Type II: PaCO2 > 50 with acidosis", "Fatigue and apnea → ventilate", "Airway protection"]),
    ch(46, "Mechanical Ventilators", "Ventilators deliver breaths with control variables (volume or pressure), trigger (time, pressure, or flow), limit, and cycle. Modes include assist-control, SIMV, pressure support, and pressure-regulated volume control. Alarms warn about high pressure, low pressure (disconnect), and apnea.", ["Volume vs. pressure control", "Trigger, limit, cycle", "A/C, SIMV, PSV", "Low-pressure alarm = disconnect"]),
    ch(47, "Physiology of Ventilatory Support", "Positive pressure ventilation raises intrathoracic pressure, which can lower venous return and cardiac output. Risks include barotrauma, volutrauma, and auto-PEEP. PEEP improves oxygenation by keeping alveoli open.", ["Positive pressure lowers venous return", "Barotrauma and volutrauma", "Auto-PEEP from air trapping", "PEEP improves oxygenation"]),
    ch(48, "Patient-Ventilator Interactions", "Patient-ventilator asynchrony (trigger, flow, and cycle asynchrony) increases work of breathing and discomfort. Solutions include adjusting sensitivity, flow, and modes, as well as appropriate sedation.", ["Asynchrony increases work", "Adjust trigger sensitivity", "Flow asynchrony", "Sedation when needed"]),
    ch(49, "Initiating and Adjusting Invasive Ventilatory Support", "Initial settings use tidal volume 6–8 mL/kg ideal body weight, rate 12–20, FiO2 100% then titrate, and PEEP 5 cm H2O. Adjust rate or tidal volume to change PaCO2; adjust FiO2 or PEEP to change PaO2.", ["VT 6–8 mL/kg IBW", "Change rate/VT for CO2", "Change FiO2/PEEP for O2", "Start FiO2 high, then titrate"]),
    ch(50, "Noninvasive Ventilation", "NIV (BiPAP, CPAP) supports breathing through a mask without intubation. It works well for COPD exacerbations and cardiogenic pulmonary edema. Contraindications include inability to protect the airway, vomiting, and facial trauma. IPAP supports ventilation; EPAP maintains alveolar recruitment.", ["Best for COPD and CHF", "IPAP vs. EPAP", "Need intact airway protection", "Mask fit matters"]),
    ch(51, "Extracorporeal Life Support", "ECMO oxygenates blood and removes CO2 outside the body for severe respiratory or cardiac failure. Venovenous (VV) ECMO supports the lungs; venoarterial (VA) supports both the heart and lungs. Risks include bleeding and clots.", ["VV ECMO = lungs", "VA ECMO = heart + lungs", "Anticoagulation needed", "Rescue therapy"]),
    ch(52, "Monitoring the Patient in the Intensive Care Unit", "ICU monitoring includes airway pressures (peak and plateau), compliance, ABGs, capnography, hemodynamics (arterial line, central venous pressure, pulmonary artery catheter), and neurological status.", ["Peak vs. plateau pressure", "Compliance calculation", "Arterial line", "CVP and PA catheters"]),
    ch(53, "Discontinuing Ventilatory Support", "Weaning starts when the underlying problem improves. Spontaneous breathing trials (SBTs) with daily sedation interruption are used. The rapid shallow breathing index (RSBI = rate ÷ tidal volume in L) under 105 predicts success. After extubation, watch for stridor.", ["Daily SBT", "RSBI < 105", "Sedation interruption", "Post-extubation stridor"]),
    ch(54, "Neonatal and Pediatric Respiratory Care", "Neonatal care includes delivery room resuscitation, oxygen targets to avoid retinopathy of prematurity, CPAP, surfactant, and special ventilators (including high-frequency oscillatory ventilation). Children's smaller airways make them more vulnerable to obstruction.", ["Avoid excess O2 in preemies", "Bubble CPAP", "HFOV", "Small airways obstruct easily"]),
    ch(55, "Health Promotion Through Patient Education", "Patient education promotes self-management: inhaler technique, smoking cessation, asthma action plans, and recognizing warning signs. Teaching considers learning domains (cognitive, psychomotor, affective), health literacy, and teach-back.", ["Teach-back confirms understanding", "Asthma action plans", "Smoking cessation", "Consider health literacy"]),
    ch(56, "Cardiopulmonary Rehabilitation", "Pulmonary rehabilitation combines exercise training, education, breathing techniques (pursed-lip breathing), nutrition, and psychosocial support. It improves exercise tolerance and quality of life in COPD, but doesn't improve lung function tests.", ["Exercise training is core", "Pursed-lip breathing", "Improves quality of life", "Doesn't improve PFTs"]),
    ch(57, "Respiratory Care in Alternative Settings", "RTs work in home care, long-term care, and sleep labs. Home care includes oxygen concentrators, liquid oxygen, portable systems, home ventilators, and CPAP. Medicare covers home oxygen when PaO2 is 55 mm Hg or less, or SpO2 88% or less.", ["Oxygen concentrators", "Home oxygen: PaO2 ≤ 55 or SpO2 ≤ 88%", "Home ventilators", "No smoking near oxygen"]),
    ch(58, "Ethics and the End of Life", "End-of-life care respects patient wishes through advance directives, DNR orders, and surrogate decision makers. Palliative care manages symptoms like dyspnea (with opioids and oxygen). Withdrawal of ventilator support follows patient or family wishes with comfort measures.", ["Advance directives", "Palliative care eases dyspnea", "Withdrawal of support", "Respect patient wishes"]),
]

